<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhuge Liang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">181–234</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Kongming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhuge Liang was the chancellor of Shu Han in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> and chief adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, whose alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> led to victory at Red Cliffs in 208. After Liu Bei's death he governed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a>, pacified the south and led five northern expeditions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>. He became the model of the wise minister in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang.jpg" alt="Zhuge Liang" width="320" height="649" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%98%8E%E4%BA%BA%E7%BB%98_%E3%80%8A%E8%AF%B8%E8%91%9B%E4%BA%AE%E5%83%8F%E3%80%8B%EF%BC%88%E5%8D%97%E8%96%B0%E6%AE%BF%E6%9C%AC%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhuge Liang's ancestral home was in Yangdu County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langya-commandery">Langya Commandery</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. Orphaned young, he was raised by Zhuge Xuan, a cousin of his father, whom he accompanied to Jing Province, where Zhuge Xuan lived with the governor Liu Biao. Zhuge Liang later lived in seclusion at Longzhong and earned the sobriquet Sleeping Dragon. The hermit Sima Hui and Xu Shu both recommended him to the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, who visited him three times. Zhuge Liang then proposed the Longzhong Plan: to secure Jing and Yi provinces, ally with Eastern Wu and resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the autumn of 208 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> invaded Jing Province. Liu Bei fled to Xiakou, was defeated at the Battle of Changban, and sent Zhuge Liang to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> to propose an alliance. Sun Quan sent Zhou Yu, Cheng Pu, Lu Su and others with 30,000 troops, and in the winter of 208 the allies won a decisive victory over Cao Cao at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-red-cliffs">Battle of Red Cliffs</a>. Defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-xiaoting">Battle of Xiaoting</a> in 222, Liu Bei fell ill and in early 223 summoned Zhuge Liang, telling him to assist the heir but to make his own decision if the heir proved incompetent.</p><p>As regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a>, Zhuge Liang refused letters from Wei officials urging Shu to submit. In 225 to 227 he led a southern campaign into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanzhong">Nanzhong</a> against rebellions, following Ma Su's advice to win the hearts of the people. In 227 he sent Liu Shan the memorial Chu Shi Biao and gathered troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a>. In 228 his army reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-qi">Mount Qi</a>, and three Wei commanderies went over to Shu. In 234 he camped more than 100,000 troops on the Wuzhang Plains, where soldiers grew crops alongside civilians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a stalemate of more than 100 days, Zhuge Liang became critically ill and died in camp, between 11 September and 10 October 234, aged 54 by East Asian reckoning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a> gave him the posthumous title Marquis Zhongwu. As governor of Yi Province he ruled by strict law and helped write the legal codes of Shu Han. The Chu Shi Biao is regarded as a masterpiece of Chinese prose. In the 14th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a> he is a near-sage of wisdom and loyalty, and temples to the Marquis of Wu stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baidicheng">Baidicheng</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhuge Liang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He sent tribute to Cao Cao and later dealt with the rival Three Kingdoms, and in 231 he marched to Beidi in support of <b>Zhuge Liang</b>'s northern campaign against Cao Wei.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The chancellor <b>Zhuge Liang</b> governed for his son Liu Shan, with strict law, irrigation works and salt and iron monopolies.</blockquote><a href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yinan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Qishan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.55,118.47&amp;year=215" aria-label="Yinan County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7544 879 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhuge Liang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7994 1149h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7994 1149h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.55,118.47&amp;year=215">What was here in 215 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
